WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.969
One dollar:

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:02.676
4.2 trillion marks.

00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:05.356
Germany, November 1923.

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:08.355
In 1914, a dollar had cost 4.2

00:00:08.365 --> 00:00:09.196
marks.

00:00:09.296 --> 00:00:13.188
In 1921, the Allies fixed the reparations

00:00:13.198 --> 00:00:16.217
bill at 132 billion gold marks.

00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:17.427
And it got worse:

00:00:17.437 --> 00:00:20.614
in January 1923, French
and Belgian soldiers

00:00:20.624 --> 00:00:22.165
marched into the Ruhr.

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:25.340
Germany printed money to pay the strikers.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.756
A loaf of bread in Berlin went

00:00:27.766 --> 00:00:30.728
from about 160 marks to 200 billion.

00:00:30.828 --> 00:00:33.512
Then, in November 1923, it stopped.

00:00:33.522 --> 00:00:33.971
How?

00:00:34.071 --> 00:00:36.104
A new currency, the Rentenmark:

00:00:36.114 --> 00:00:38.029
one for a trillion old marks, with

00:00:38.039 --> 00:00:40.002
its supply strictly fixed.
